    {
      "slide": 8,
      "point": "'Unit economics' is just the 18 EUR price. No gross margin, CAC, churn or LTV — all flagged gaps. Investors cannot compute payback.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Add margin per box, churn and CAC (estimates labeled as such); state what is unknown and how the raise funds measuring it."
    },
    {
      "slide": 9,
      "point": "Ask lacks a use-of-funds breakdown, runway projection and milestones. 'Construction and equipment' for 300k EUR is unitemized; no post-raise survival math.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Itemize capex vs working capital, state months of runway and the subscriber-capacity milestone the second roastery unlocks."
    },
    {
      "slide": 7,
      "point": "Competitors named but no differentiation. Kyiv City Roastery already sells subscriptions — the deck proves the model works for others, not why BeanBox wins.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Add a 'why we win' argument: curation, pricing vs cafes, retention advantage — anything defensible beyond 'market is validated'."
    },
    {
      "slide": 2,
      "point": "'Validated product-market fit' overclaims. 400 subscribers with no churn or growth-curve data could be plateaued early volume, not PMF.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "fix": "Show monthly subscriber growth and churn; soften to 'early traction' until retention data exists."
    },
    {
      "slide": 3,
      "point": "'Data-driven growth' and 'efficient acquisition' are unsupported — no CAC, channels or founder track records beyond job titles.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "fix": "Include CAC from the marketer's own data and one concrete result per founder; cut adjectives without numbers."
    },
    {
      "slide": 4,
      "point": "Market slide has no numbers: no Kyiv subscription TAM/SAM/SOM. 'Dense competition validates demand' is weak logic, and market sources are medium-confidence.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "fix": "Estimate the Kyiv specialty-subscription base, cite Ukraine's ~300–400M USD market with a sourcing caveat, define the obtainable segment."
    },
    {
      "slide": 6,
      "point": "No build-vs-outsource case: contract roasting could add capacity cheaper than 300k EUR capex. No mitigation shown for Kyiv war/logistics risk.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "fix": "Compare owning vs contract roasting on margin and quality control; add a risk slide covering operations in Kyiv."
    }
  ]
}
